EXT. MANGROVE RIVER - RAFT - DUSK

The river is brown glass between walls of knotted roots. Mosquitoes hang in the amber light. A flat raft of lashed bamboo drifts without a pole, carried by a current too slow to feel.

KOFI (30s, bare-chested, sunburnt) lies on his back with a torn shirt over his face. NAOMI (30s, cropped hair, cracked lips) kneels at the edge, trailing a hand in the water. HAKON (70s, white beard, wool cap, still in a ruined parka) sits cross-legged, tying and untying the same knot. RUSLAN (8, too-big life vest) watches a heron on a root.

Nobody speaks. Somewhere a frog begins and stops.

Kofi lifts the shirt. Squints at the sky. Lets it fall.

Naomi draws her hand from the water and looks at her fingers, wrinkled and white. She wipes them on her thigh.

Hakon pulls the knot tight. Studies it. Loosens it.

Ruslan holds out a cupped palm toward the heron. The bird lifts, unhurried, and flaps over the trees. He and Naomi both follow it with their eyes. Kofi and Hakon do not.

The raft turns a bend. Ahead, the mangroves part on a long gray mud flat. Far off, a roofline. Maybe a boat shed. Maybe a mirage.

All four see it. All four go still. Nobody says a word, in case the word makes it vanish.
